      "narrative": "Mara Venn's eyes narrow at your question. She tilts her head, studying you with renewed interest.\n\n\"The lockwright?\" She drums her fingers on the table. \"That'd be\u2014\"\n\nShe stops. Her gaze slides past you, toward the bar. Toward Toran Vex.\n\nThe clerk has gone very still behind his ledger. His ink-stained fingers are frozen mid-page. He's not breathing.\n\nMara's mouth opens, then closes. She shakes her head slowly.\n\n\"You know what,\" she says, picking up her mug again, \"I can't rightly recall. There've been a few over the years. The city sends someone when the mechanism seizes up. Could've been any of them.\"\n\nShe's lying. You can feel it in the way her words come too smooth, too casual \u2014 the rhythm of someone who decided mid-sentence to shut a door.\n\nBut you pushed too directly, and the door slammed before you got a name.\n\nToran Vex exhales quietly behind the bar and turns a page in his ledger, the sound deliberate and loud in the silence.\n\nMara takes a long drink, then sets the mug down empty. She meets your eyes.\n\n\"Funny thing about locks,\" she says. \"The ones worth knowing about are the ones nobody's supposed to open. So maybe the better question isn't *who* kept the lock \u2014 but *why* someone wanted it filed shut in the first place.\"\n\nShe watches you, waiting to see if you'll take the thread she's offered \u2014 or keep pushing at the one she's just cut."
    },
