      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the warm lamplight and the smell of old ale. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp and cool, carrying the thin scent of wet stone and distant smoke.\n\nYou cross the muddy yard, boots squelching in the churned earth. The Ash Gate rises ahead of you against the grey sky: a cracked black arch, tall enough to pass a wagon through, standing alone in a patch of bare ground like a tooth that's outlasted the jaw.\n\nUp close, it's worse than it looked from the tavern window.\n\nThe black stone is pitted and scarred. Moss clings to the base where rainwater pools. And there \u2014 the lock mechanism. A heavy iron housing set into the left pillar, its face marred by fresh scratches. Bright metal gleams where a file has bitten into the iron, chewing away at the wards inside. Someone did this recently. Within the last day, maybe two.\n\nA body lies crumpled at the base of the right pillar.\n\nAn old man. Grey beard. Wet clothes clinging to still limbs. A leather satchel lies a few feet away, its strap torn.\n\nThe air around the gate is still. Too still. No birds. No wind. Just the drip of water from the arch's curve and the silence of a place that wants to be left alone."
    },
