      "narrative": "Your voice echoes off the pitted black stone \u2014 \"Old Bram! Old Bram!\" \u2014 and fades into the damp air.\n\nNothing.\n\nNo answering call. No shuffle of feet. Just the drip of water from the arch and the distant creak of a sign swinging somewhere back in town.\n\nYou wait. You listen.\n\nThe silence stretches. Your ears strain, catching every small sound \u2014 the rustle of your own cloak, the soft patter of moisture beading on stone, the faint hum of... something. Almost there. A whisper at the edge of hearing, like a word spoken underwater. You hold your breath, leaning toward the gate, but it slips away before you can grasp it. Just the wind. Just your imagination.\n\nThe dead man at the pillar hasn't moved. Of course he hasn't. He's dead.\n\nBut as you stand there, listening to nothing, you feel it \u2014 a prickle at the back of your neck. The sense that something *is* listening back. Not from beyond the gate. From *behind* you.\n\nYou turn.\n\nThe yard is empty. The Copper Finch's windows are dark rectangles. Nothing moves.\n\nBut the feeling doesn't fade."
