      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch, scanning the churned mud beneath the arch. Your eyes trace the ground methodically \u2014 the dark patches where water has pooled, the shallow impressions of boots, the scattered pebbles washed clean by the morning damp.\n\nBut it all looks the same. Uniform. Just mud.\n\nYou run your fingers through a rut, feeling for anything out of place \u2014 a seam, a hardness that shouldn't be there, a disturbance in the earth. Nothing. Just cold, wet soil that squelches between your fingers.\n\nIf there's something hidden here, the mud has swallowed it well. Or there was never anything here to find.\n\nThe dead man's satchel lies a few feet away, its torn strap dark with moisture. The lock mechanism gleams dully above you, its fresh file marks catching what little light the grey sky offers. The body itself \u2014 Old Bram, presumably \u2014 lies still against the pillar, his wet clothes clinging to the stone.\n\nThe gate looms. Silent. Unrevealing.\n\nBehind you, the feeling of being watched hasn't faded. You glance back again.\n\nStill nothing. Just empty yard and shuttered windows.\n\nBut the prickle on your neck doesn't go away."
